# Console platformer, 1985 -- evocations, not the originals.
#
# The originals run on an NES APU: two pulse channels with four selectable duty cycles, a
# triangle, a noise channel and a hardware sweep unit. POKEY has none of that. It has one
# square (distortion 5), several polynomial textures, and an envelope that rewrites
# registers on a timer.
#
# So two things differ audibly no matter how these are tuned. There is no duty cycle here,
# which is where the original's reedy quality comes from; and AUDF is 8-bit against the
# NES's 11, so the top of a fast rise is coarse -- at AUDF 35 one step is 21Hz, against
# 1.4Hz down at 140.
#
# What does carry across for free: a constant AUDF delta gives a hyperbolic pitch curve, so
# the rise accelerates on its own -- which is close to what the NES sweep unit does by
# decreasing the period geometrically.

bank   Platformer
clock  1512000
tick   220

category Movement

sound Jump
audf  140 1 -5 22
audf  35 1 0 22
audc  170 1 0 26
audc  170 2 -1 10
amp   0.45
note  Two octaves up in a fifth of a second, then a fade. The rise accelerates on its own
note  because AUDF is a divider, which is what makes a constant delta the right shape here
note  rather than a compromise.

sound Jump, big
audf  170 1 -6 24
audf  32 1 0 20
audc  170 1 0 28
audc  170 2 -1 10
amp   0.48
note  The same gesture starting lower and travelling further. Size in a platformer is
note  almost always pitch.

sound Stomp
audf  60 1 +22 8
audc  138 1 -1 8
amp   0.5
note  Noise falling fast. Everything about a stomp is in the first fiftieth of a second.

category Pickups

sound Coin
audf  50 1 0 4
audf  33 1 0 14
audc  170 1 0 18
amp   0.42
note  Two flat notes, the second a fifth above and much longer. The most copied sound
note  effect ever written, and it is two numbers.

sound Power up
audf  150 1 -4 30
audc  170 1 0 24
audc  170 2 -1 8
amp   0.42
note  A long climb. Where the coin is two notes, this is one continuous rise -- which is
note  how a two-note flourish and a transformation end up feeling different.

sound One up
audf  90 1 0 5
audf  70 1 0 5
audf  55 1 0 5
audf  45 1 0 12
audc  170 1 0 27
amp   0.45
note  Four rising notes held flat. An arpeggio rather than a sweep, which is what makes it
note  read as a fanfare instead of an effect.

category States

sound Brick break
audf  40 1 +18 10
audc  138 1 -1 10
amp   0.45
note  The 17-bit poly is proper noise, and a short burst of it is every breaking thing in
note  every game.

sound Pipe
audf  40 1 +10 20
audc  73 1 0 20
amp   0.4
note  Distortion 2 -- the 5- and 4-bit polys together, the roughest pitched texture the
note  chip has. Descending, because you are going down.

sound Death
audf  60 1 -8 6
audf  30 1 +7 30
audc  170 1 0 36
amp   0.5
note  Up sharply, then a long fall. The little rise first is what makes it a lurch rather
note  than a slide, and it is the whole character of the sound.
